Looking closely at your controller implementation, there are two major architectural issues here:
 * The Double-Conversion Anti-Pattern (Unnecessary ObjectMapper usage)
 * The Manual Instantiation of ObjectMapper
1. Do you really need ObjectMapper in this controller?
In most clean architectures, NO.
Look at the lifecycle of your request:
 * Spring MVC automatically uses Jackson under the hood to deserialize the incoming raw JSON payload into Tmf656EventPayload jsonPayload (via @RequestBody).
 * Inside the method, you immediately call objectMapper.writeValueAsString(jsonPayload) to serialize it right back into a JSON string just to pass it to loggingService and queueService.enqueueHttpEvent(rawJsonString, ...).
Incoming JSON -> [Spring Jackson Deserializes] -> Tmf656EventPayload -> [ObjectMapper Serializes BACK] -> rawJsonString

This causes unnecessary CPU and memory overhead on every incoming HTTP request.
2. Better Alternatives
Alternative A: Pass the typed object to queueService (Best Practice)
Instead of enqueuing raw JSON strings, let queueService accept the strongly-typed DTO Tmf656EventPayload (or a domain model).
 * Advantage: Eliminates ObjectMapper from the controller entirely and keeps your domain strongly typed.
@PostMapping(consumes = MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE)
public ResponseEntity<Void> receiveClientListenerEvent(@Valid @RequestBody Tmf656EventPayload jsonPayload) {
    try {
        log.info("[TMF-656] Inbound HTTP POST event received for eventId={}", jsonPayload.getEventId());
        
        serviceProblemService.registerOrUpdate(jsonPayload);
        queueService.enqueueHttpEvent(jsonPayload, AlarmSource.EATON);

        return ResponseEntity.noContent().build();
    } catch (Exception e) {
        log.error("[TMF-656] Error processing listener event: {}", e.getMessage(), e);
        return ResponseEntity.badRequest().build();
    }
}

Alternative B: Accept @RequestBody String rawJson + Manually Parse (If raw JSON is strictly required)
If downstream services or auditing systems strictly demand the exact, unmodified raw JSON payload string as received over the wire:
 * Accept @RequestBody String rawJsonPayload in the method signature.
 * Convert it to Tmf656EventPayload once using an injected ObjectMapper (or validate it).
 * This preserves the original wire payload without formatting distortions caused by round-trip deserialization/serialization.
3. If you DO need ObjectMapper, how should it be fixed?
If you decide to keep ObjectMapper in this class or another service, do not instantiate it with new.
The comment in your code notes:
> * Avoids "No qualifying bean of type ObjectMapper" startup failure
> 
This error occurs when Spring Boot's JacksonAutoConfiguration is missing, disabled, or excluded. Fix the root cause by providing a centralized @Bean configuration:
@Configuration
public class JacksonConfig {

    @Bean
    public ObjectMapper objectMapper() {
        return Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilder.json()
                .modules(new JavaTimeModule())
                .featuresToDisable(SerializationFeature.WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS)
                .build();
    }
}

Then, allow Lombok's @RequiredArgsConstructor to inject it cleanly:
@RestController
@RequiredArgsConstructor
@RequestMapping("/api/alarms")
@ConditionalOnWebApplication(type = Type.SERVLET)
public class Tmf656ListenerController {

    private final AlarmQueueService queueService;
    private final Tmf656ServiceProblemService serviceProblemService;
    private final AlarmLoggingService loggingService;
    private final ObjectMapper objectMapper; // Clean constructor injection via Lombok
    
    // ...
}

Summary Recommendation
 * Remove the serialization step: Refactor queueService.enqueueHttpEvent(...) to accept Tmf656EventPayload directly.
 * Remove ObjectMapper from the Controller: Controllers should only route requests, delegate business logic, and return HTTP responses.
 * Provide a @Bean ObjectMapper globally if other services in your microservice need custom serialization rules.
